[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm trying to work with binomial coefficients on <code>ENat</code>: that is, the mathematically reasonable function <code>choose : ENat -&gt; Nat -&gt; ENat</code>  with <code>⊤.choose 0 = 1</code>,<code>⊤.choose (r + 1) = ⊤</code> and <code>(↑n).choose r = ↑(n.choose r)</code>. </p>\n<p>This is almost a special case of  <code>Ring.choose</code>, but it doesn't quite work; here's why. </p>\n<p>At the moment, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=BinomialRing#doc\">docs#BinomialRing</a> is defined as follows, with <code>multichoose</code> as a field but not <code>choose</code>. </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BinomialRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Pow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">toIsAddTorsionFree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAddTorsionFree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A multichoose function, giving the quotient of Pochhammer evaluations by factorials. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The `n`th ascending Pochhammer polynomial evaluated at any element is divisible by `n!` -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">factorial_nsmul_multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factorial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ascPochhammer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">smeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And then <code>Ring.choose</code> is defined in terms of <code>multichoose</code>. </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommGroupWithOne</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Pow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">BinomialRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The instance <code>BinomialRing ENat</code> is easy to define, but <code>Ring.choose</code> doesn't work for <code>ENat</code>, because it needs a group structure due to the subtraction. </p>\n<p>I believe this could be fixed with a refactor, where <code>BinomialRing</code> is defined with a slightly stronger typeclass assumption, and <code>choose</code> as a structure field, like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BinomialRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoidWithOne</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Pow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">toIsAddTorsionFree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsAddTorsionFree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A multichoose function, giving the quotient of Pochhammer evaluations by factorials. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The `n`th ascending Pochhammer polynomial evaluated at any element is divisible by `n!` -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">factorial_nsmul_multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factorial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ascPochhammer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">smeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A choose function -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- `multichoose` and `choose` interact correctly. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose_add_one_eq_choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">multichoose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This will allow for nearly all the same API, except that <code>BinomialRing R</code> can no longer be supplied unless <code>R</code> has a <code>One</code>. At the moment, I don't believe that  the above change would break anything, though it would slightly weaken the scope of the instance <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=instBinomialRingOfModuleNNRat#doc\">docs#instBinomialRingOfModuleNNRat</a> . </p>\n<p>Does anyone have any thoughts on this change? Pinging <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"630477\">@Scott Carnahan</span> , the original author.</p>",
        "id": 591877506,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Nelson",
        "timestamp": 1777490093
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok - it seems that this doesn't really work - the axiom relating <code>multichoose</code> and <code>choose</code> would still allow <code>choose r n</code> to behave incorrectly for <code>r &lt; n</code> in the case of <code>ENat</code>.</p>\n<p>I think that redefining <code>Ring.choose</code> in such a way that <code>Nat.choose</code> and <code>ENat.choose</code> are actually special cases would be worthwhile.  Maybe this needs some use of <code>OrderedSub</code> and related typeclasses.</p>",
        "id": 591892249,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Nelson",
        "timestamp": 1777494457
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think your idea of adding a <code>choose</code> field is excellent, but I haven’t thought about the correct compatibility conditions.</p>",
        "id": 591906687,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1777499931
    }
]